hi there i guess it started i'll start  up my  uh microphone as well uh been packing  here to get ready to leave for uh  portugal and spain my trip  i wanted to do a video on the subject of  reading  it has come up on my blog it comes up  every now and again for a variety of  reasons  first of all reading is a very effective  way  to  improve your language skills in your own  language or in a foreign language  stephen krashen whom i often quote  believes that extensive silent reading  is the most powerful way to learn  a language or to improve in a language  i've had comments on my blog john  fatheringham for one igor the macedonian  with  all with slightly different perspectives  but nevertheless supporting the fact  that reading is  a very powerful way to include to  improve your language skills  now reading comes up as well uh not only  as part of language learning but as part  of the whole discussion about literacy  and i posted in a comment to my blog  some of the reports that are out there  saying that um you know 40 of the people  in  certainly in the united states or canada  and perhaps other industrialized nations  are functionally illiterate  uh  but a lot of this comes from the sort of  literacy advocate people and i'm very  skeptical about their numbers because i  think the whole issue of who is  functionally illiterate is really quite  subjective  and the numbers can vary from you know  30 to 40 to 50 percent and so forth it  doesn't really matter  the fact is that that there is a  perception  and we hear this from university  professors and others that the graduates  that they that are now entering  university read less and less well  now again it's common in human history  for the present generation to consider  that the young or the older generation  to consider that the younger generation  is no good and stuff so there's a little  bit of that certainly but let's assume  that we have a problem  and that it's difficult to measure the  problem and different people will try to  make uh you know different uh different  or  you know point out that the the the the  scope of the problem can can  sometimes be difficult to grab but the  problem is probably quite large and and  i i do believe that the more literate  you are  the better you use language the better  off you do  socially professionally and so forth in  our society now for some people that may  not matter there are many people who are  happily illiterate they have a job that  they can do  their loving family members have friends  and have a good time  but i i think for a lot of people  there  it's it is possible to trace a  correlation between low literacy  high unemployment levels high  incarceration rates  alcoholism you name it a lot of ills  seem to be attached with low literacy  levels so it's probably a good thing to  help people become better readers  now  i have mentioned before that i've been  on this  list serve put out by the national  institute for literacy or something and  and a lot of the literacy teachers are  more interested in social  uh change than they are in teaching  people how to read and and they're all  caught up in in the sort of  ideology of pedagogy in other words you  know literacy is a social construct and  really if someone can uh you know uh use  text to speech then we may be able to  finesse the whole literacy issue  altogether and it's just another way for  people with power in society to put  other people down and so  all these kind of irrelevant things  uh  i think the simple  uh  issue is that people do better in terms  of what most people want to achieve in  their lives they do better if they can  read well  i think that is probably true  so whose fault is it that so many people  seem to be unable to read and possibly  we have more people unable to read now  or to read well now than several  generations ago  uh it's certainly partly the  individual's fault and certainly to some  extent a factor of the family  environment it's certainly uh possibly  uh influenced by television and computer  games all of this is possible  but there's one thing to me that is  inescapable and that is that the schools  have the kids  for  you know five six hours a day  five days a week for 12 years  so  if kids graduate from high school and  cannot read there is no escaping the  responsibility of the school system  whatever other factors can and do  influence  how well someone reads  if i have someone in my charge  for 12 years  five hours a day five days a week and i  can also assign homework to that person  uh assign tasks to that person  watch what they're doing help them  correct them do whatever and at the end  of the day they can't read very well  that's my fault you can't escape the  responsibility of the school system and  i believe that the schools would be far  better off if they allowed people to sit  there and read silently  than do what they now do where teachers  just prattle on about all kinds of stuff  i remember that from my school  days uh i don't know what she or she is  saying half the time we're more focused  not fooling around  uh unless the teacher captures our  attention and our and our interest  uh they're entranced into various social  propagandas you know save the whales  uh you know mommy has two parents  they're both men or both women  uh any of these things which might be  wonderful causes to promote but  we shouldn't be promoting these kinds of  sort of  values however  wonderful the teacher thinks they are  if the kids can't read  and to get people to read let them read  things that they are interested in i  think that has to be  a major part of it and when i read on  these literacy list serves about  teachers who are going to impose this  poetry because they thought it's so  wonderful and then they're going to ask  the class  what they thought of it and whether they  can explain it and stuff and all of that  just  destroys reading and crashing has said  it that the sort of reading strategies  approach is leads to poorer reading  and if we can somehow provide kids with  reading opportunities provide them with  books encourage them let them read  all of this will should lead to better  reading  the other thing i wanted to let's see  what was there  the other issue with reading of course  in so far as language learning is  concerned is this debate about we've had  about graded readers  and igor in macedonia and i don't by the  way igor think that everybody in  macedonia is a shepherd  uh i don't think that you sit on the  hillside and blow and play your flute  while the sheep wander around otherwise  you wouldn't be on the internet  uh but he's a  a very strong crash tonight all you need  to is read and therefore graded readers  are very important  i believe that if you can  help grade the material for people  that's fine  and to some extent we do that at link  because we measure the unknown words we  measure you know difficulty based on  different criteria  but the main thing is the interest and  if a person is interested in the subject  they will fight their way through  difficult content and if the person is  not interested  it doesn't matter how simple the the  text is it's he's not communicating  because you have to engage the emotions  of the learner  and every time i have seen graded  readers which i found for russian you  know simple russian stories written by  someone about you know  sasha whose friend arrives from germany  and they visit leningrad and here's this  and that monument i have no interest in  that whatsoever no matter how  interesting they try to make it it's not  real it's not something that a russian  person wrote for a russian person so uh  yeah on the other hand as i've said we  now have the ability to record  conversations and then transcribe them  and they will tend to have less  difficulty in terms of vocabulary use  and so there are a number of strategies  that you can use in order to keep it  interesting and yet keep it accessible  to the learner graded readers as in the  penguin readers simplified versions of  robinson crusoe i've never been a fan of  but i have nothing against those and let  other people do them i prefer to have a  lot of new words and that's one of the  reasons we develop link so that you  don't have to look up every third word  it's almost there for you as you're  reading through it you have an  opportunity to work the words and  phrases and i do believe contrary to  crash that some degree of vocabulary  study i've said it 5 10 max no more than  that and some degree of output not  necessarily in the early stages but  later on  more and more output so you can  start to notice what you're missing  because  through your reading you get in a groove  you certain things you just don't notice  until you start to use the language and  then you make certain mistakes and now  you can start looking for those things  and you start to notice better so i've  rambled a bit on reading i'm going to  run out of time here i'm heading off to  portugal and spain we'll see if i can do  any more of these later on thank you for  listening bye for now
 

